## Decision

Replace [decision 0001](/kb/decisions/0001-dedupe-by-subject) with cause-based triage.
Keep that accepted record intact as history. Its subject-only rule and the
[triage page](/kb/triage/dedupe) describe the old policy; their rendered review dates flag
them as stale. Matching subjects can hide different causes, and different subjects can
describe the same cause.

The [incident page](/kb/incidents/4471) says one issue and one queued fix. Its rendered
table contradicts that claim. Treat the prose as an incomplete historical account.
The export ends on 4 October; this review does not establish the live desk's state after that date.

<Callout kind="warn">
  Split the merged ticket into separate parser and booking-form hint tickets. A known
  parser defect does not cover the form hint defect. A queued fix is not proof of resolution.
</Callout>

## What the code and export show

<ReviewSummary />

<CauseTable ticket={4471} />

Counts come from distinct report IDs, filtered to the merged ticket, then classified by
the tenant directory and `causeOf` in `src/lib/kb/causes.ts`. Locale ordering comes from
`Intl.DateTimeFormat(...).formatToParts()`. The browser locale and `meta.intakeTag` record
intake's guess, not the tenant's date contract. Do not use those tags as issue counts.

### Parser: day-first dates read month first

The shared parser assumes month-first input for day-first tenants. For example, report
`R-41203` says 2 December became 12 February. `R-41205` says 3 October became 10 March,
even though intake tagged it as hint. Reports through app and email, plus booking-v2
forms, belong here. This is the majority cause represented by the original ticket.

Fix the parser's input contract and preserve the tenant's intended date through booking,
invite and reminder generation. Validate with ambiguous dates and day values above twelve.
Do not rely on a browser locale or a general claim that all European locales are alike.

### Hint: booking-v3 tells day-first users to enter month first

For form submissions from `alder.physio` (`de-DE`), `copper.studio` (`es-ES`) and
`pale.harbour` (`pt-PT`), booking-v3 prints `MM/DD/YYYY` while its date interpretation
uses the tenant's day-first order. The hint and reader disagree. This needs its own fix,
separate from the shared parser. `R-41230` and `R-41251` show why intake's parser tag
does not settle the cause.

Make the displayed hint and form reader agree with the tenant's locale. Check the hint,
submitted value, saved booking and generated invite together. App and email reports
from those same tenants still classify as parser.

## The desk kept merging

<QueueTimeline ticket={4471} />

Reports continued arriving through the end of the export. Merging a report into a known
ticket does not repair a booking or remove the need to investigate it. Customers describe
missed appointments, manual corrections and workarounds; report totals measure reports,
not all affected people or bookings. Calendar invites reach about 300,000 people in
`sched-prod`, but this export does not identify the affected product for each report or
justify treating that entire population as affected.

## Triage from now on

1. Look up the tenant, its locale and booking form. Read the report body and channel.
2. Compare causes. Merge only when the evidence supports the same defect; a shared
   subject alone is insufficient. Unknown tenants stay unplaced until investigated.
3. Keep a distinct report ID once, retain the source IDs and count it under its cause.
4. Keep anomalous time-related descriptions visible for investigation. Do not infer a
   timezone fix from these descriptions alone.
5. Track both tickets separately and verify each fix against the affected booking flow.
   Recheck subsequent reports before marking either cause resolved.

